KALIYUGA: THE BLEEDING CROWN
Full Story Script (v5)
Format: BOLD is a speaker, italics are direction, ~~strikethrough~~ is Sanjaya's narration being corrected on screen. The Mani holds power and souls. It does not answer questions.

PROLOGUE: THE SCROLLS
Painted scroll panels, dark gold and ash. A blind narrator's voice over rough brushwork.
SANJAYA (V.O.): I am blind. Everything I tell you was shown to me. Let it be written.
Scroll 1: The Child.
SANJAYA: In the age when the asuras were winning heaven, an old god gave everything he had left to a child not yet born. The mother, Suvarna, said yes, and died in the giving. The child was named Rudra. A gem opened in his brow, the Mani, and the boy could hold more power than the sky.
A sage in white kneels over the infant. KAALA-CHAKRA, kind, patient.
KAALA-CHAKRA: You are a weapon the gods made. I will make you a good one.
Scroll 2: The Brothers.
Two boys on a riverbank. NACHIKETA, the younger, bandages a bird's wing. RUDRA, older, drills with a staff.
NACHIKETA: Why do you always hit things?
RUDRA (young): So you can fix them.
Scroll 3: The Lesson.
Rudra, age fifteen, stands over a captured asura boy. Kaala-Chakra holds out a blade.
RUDRA: He is a child, Father. Should I?
KAALA-CHAKRA: A weapon does not ask. It strikes.
Rudra's hand shakes. He does it.
SANJAYA: That was the last time he asked.
Scroll 4: The Fall of the Father.
Shumbha and Nishumbha, asura twin kings, pour gold into Kaala-Chakra's hands. A throne is carved in his name.
SANJAYA: The twins offered him a seat on the Council of Seven. The war fed his fortune, so he stopped wanting it to end. He told his son the gods were tyrants. His son believed him.
Rudra, in black armor, the asuras' commander.

THE OPENING (playable, about three minutes in the demo)
Scene 1: The Rampage. Playable.
Heaven's gate burns. Rudra tears through divine guards. The first fight is pure power: no posture, no pressure, just victory.
SANJAYA: ~~The gods had killed his brother. He answered.~~
Kaala-Chakra's voice over the battle, from a distance.
KAALA-CHAKRA (V.O.): The gods took Nachiketa, Rudra. Burn them all.
Scene 2: The Night of Ash, as Rudra remembers it. Playable.
A black fortress on a hill. Torches, dark windows, armored shapes on the walls. No lights. No families.
RUDRA: Father?
KAALA-CHAKRA (V.O.): They are in there.
Rudra raises both hands. The sky bends toward him. The Brahmashira, a white fire the colour of a held breath.
White. Silence.
Scene 3: The Duel. Playable, unwinnable.
Vishnu descends in light. He is not angry. Rudra attacks with everything; Vishnu blocks, deflects, never strikes back until the end.
VISHNU: Rudra. Go and look.
Rudra does not hear it. The player does. A blow shatters the Mani. Six shards scatter, a seventh falls from the sky. Rudra falls toward black water.
Scene 4: Black screen.
A single lantern, dim, growing. A woman's voice.
SAVITRI: Get up. You owe a lot of people.

THE HUB: THE VAITARANI
The river of the dead. Fog, ash, a ferry. Savitri's lantern is the only warm light in the game. When Rudra dies in battle, he wakes here and she hauls him in. Her lantern dims a little each time.
SAVITRI: Every time you fall, it costs me. Don't fall.

CHAPTER ONE: THE CHARNEL PLAINS (Debt to the Dead)
A field of rusted chariots, bone mountains, burning pyres. The dead of both sides lie together.
SANJAYA: Rudra, the betrayed, climbed from the river. He sought the god who had broken him. The god sat on the stolen throne of heaven.
Savitri's lantern flickers over a pile of bones.
RUDRA: Who are you?
SAVITRI: The ferrywoman. You will need a boat.
RUDRA: I do not need a guide.
SAVITRI: You need a lantern. The shards pull toward you, and the way up is full of things that are not what they seem.
RUDRA: What do you want?
SAVITRI: One thing. At the end, you answer one question. Will you still be here?
He does not understand. He nods.
Mini-Boss: Dundubhi, the War-Drum Behemoth. No dialogue. A war-beast with a bronze drum for a chest. Bait it into the chariot pillars.
Boss: Vrikodara, the Iron Rakshasa.
He stands at the Ash-Gate, huge, furnace-eyed. A wolf-belly Seat, the office of Hunger.
VRIKODARA: Rudra. You are alive. Of course you are.
RUDRA: Brother. Stand aside.
VRIKODARA: My wife was a healer in the Shishira camp. My son held the lamp for her. Do you remember the Shishira?
RUDRA: There was no camp. A fortress of killers.
VRIKODARA: Then take the shard and look.
Fight. Phase 1: chained pillars and a vacuum maw. Phase 2: he swallows the chains and grows, slag splitting the arena.
As Rudra pulls the shard, Vrikodara's eyes clear.
VRIKODARA: You did not know, brother. I could never hate you. That was what the hunger was for.
He dies. Shard #1 floods Rudra: a healer's tent, a Rakshasa child holding a lamp, the lamp going out.
RUDRA: That could be any camp.
SAVITRI: (quietly) It could.
SANJAYA: ~~The fortress of killers~~ was a camp.
The strikethrough is the first correction. Rudra stares at it.
Ferry Scene I
Savitri rows. Rudra sits across from her, the shard in his fist.
SAVITRI: My daughter Ila had a sparrow once. A broken wing. She sat with it three nights. It died anyway. She asked me why we fix things that die.
Choices:
[Say nothing] Savitri continues: "I said so they don't die alone."
[Ask "What did she say?"] "That's a good reason."
[Say "I'm sorry."] "Save it. You will need it later, for someone who can use it."

CHAPTER TWO: THE DROWNED LOTUS OF PATALA (Debt to the Living World)
Bioluminescent caverns, violet poison. Savitri's lantern is essential. In the mist, only her light shows what is real.
SANJAYA: The god of heaven cursed him, and left him to rot.
Mini-Boss: Karkotaka, the Mirror-Scale Viper. Four reflections, one real. The lantern shows which.
Boss: Kadruja, the Thousand-Hooded Matriarch.
She coils around a pillar in a stepwell, blind, eyes sewn shut. The poison has twisted her.
KADRUJA: The Undying One. My youngest were in the Shishira. The truce took them as hostages and kept them with the healers. I have not found them in three thousand years.
RUDRA: I did not know.
KADRUJA: No one ever does.
Fight. Phase 1: venom curtains. Phase 2: she sheds her skin, seven hoods fire stone-beams, eggs spawn.
The choice. Carry the eggs out under fire, or crush them.
Saved: "They hatch. Faintly, in the dark, small voices." Kadruja gives up the shard and lives. A Naga flotilla appears in the final act.
Crushed: She dies screaming. The Nagas are lost.
Shard #2 floods Rudra: Vishnu above him, the duel, the god's face calm.
VISHNU (memory): Rudra. Go and look.
Rudra hears it now, three thousand years late.
RUDRA: He said it.
SAVITRI: He did.
RUDRA: Then why did he break me?
SAVITRI: Ask him when you find him.
SANJAYA: ~~Cursed by Vishnu.~~ Stopped by Vishnu.
Ferry Scene II
SAVITRI: When Ila was ten, the healers would not treat asura wounded in the same tent as gods. She did it anyway. She said a wound has no side.
Choices:
[Say nothing] "She was braver than I was."
[Ask "Did they punish her?"] "They tried. She was too useful."
[Say "She sounds good."] "She was."

CHAPTER THREE: THE FORGE OF VISHWAKARMA (Debt of Labor and Oaths)
A clockwork foundry inside a hollowed volcano, run by enslaved smiths. Furnace-smoke glows faintly blue.
SANJAYA: The war for heaven raged on.
Mini-Boss: Tarkshya-Yantra, the Brass Garuda. A four-winged clockwork raptor, diving bombs and razor feathers.
After the fight, Rudra frees a crew of smiths from their chains. Their foreman, old and burnt, stares at his broken mace.
FOREMAN: We can mend that. They told us the Undying One carries our tithe. We thought it was a story.
They reforge the mace. On the haft, a maker's mark: Kaala-Chakra's.
Rudra runs his thumb over it.
RUDRA: My father made this.
SAVITRI: (not looking at him) I know.
Boss: Shakti-Kavacha, the Unyielding Solar General.
A peerless spearman, golden armor grown into his flesh.
SHAKTI-KAVACHA: Rudra. Good. I have wanted this fight.
RUDRA: You were Vishnu's man.
SHAKTI-KAVACHA: I was an oath's man. My wounded were in the Shishira. Not as soldiers. As patients.
Fight. Phase 1: pure duel, armor cuts normal damage by seventy percent, shatter his posture. Phase 2: armor cracked, he rips spears from his own back and wields solar plasma.
He dies standing, handing over his spear.
SHAKTI-KAVACHA: I swore an oath to a lie. You swore one to your grief. Look, Rudra. Look at what the furnace burns.
Shard #3: the pyre. A shrouded body. Kaala-Chakra's hand on Rudra's shoulder.
KAALA-CHAKRA (memory): The gods took Nachiketa. Do not unwrap him. Remember him as he was.
Young Rudra reaches for the shroud. His father's hand closes on his wrist.
KAALA-CHAKRA (memory): Please.
Rudra lets go.
RUDRA: I never lifted the shroud.
SANJAYA: ~~The war for heaven.~~ The harvest.
Ferry Scene III
SAVITRI: Ila wrote me letters from the camp. The last one said the Council had moved more families inside the walls. For our protection, they said.
She does not look at Rudra.
SAVITRI: She was proud of that. She thought it meant they cared.
Choices:
[Say nothing] The river runs on.
[Ask "When did it arrive?"] "The week before."
[Say "I'm sorry."] "You said that."

CHAPTER FOUR: THE WEEPING BANYAN OF DANDAKA (Debt of Truth)
A continent-sized banyan grows upside-down from the clouds, roots dripping golden sap and trapped memories. A twilight jungle of lights.
Mini-Boss: Maricha, the Golden Phantom Stag. Two-headed, antlers of mirrors. The lantern shows which is real.
Among the golden roots, faces flicker: the dead of the Shishira. Rudra walks through them like a man walking through a graveyard.
He stops at a girl's face, sixteen, a healer's apprentice. Her jaw.
RUDRA: (turning) Savitri.
She does not move.
RUDRA: You knew.
SAVITRI: From the first hour.
RUDRA: Why did you help me?
SAVITRI: Because you are the only one who can get to the top of this mountain. And the top is where they are keeping her.
Rudra draws the mace. Lowers it. Falls to one knee.
RUDRA: I will stop. I will not climb.
SAVITRI: No.
RUDRA: I killed your child.
SAVITRI: Yes. Finish it.
Long silence. He gets up.
SANJAYA: I must stop. I was given my words. I did not see the camp. I did not see the lights. I told you what the court showed me.
His narration burns from the screen. Words rewrite themselves.
Boss: Surpanakha-Maya, Weaver of Ten Thousand Griefs.
An ancient Rakshasi, mutilated by the gods, who weaves the dream-banyan. Her daughter died in the Shishira.
SURPANAKHA-MAYA: You keep asking what the god did to you. Ask what you did to me.
Fight. Phase 1: razor karma-threads strung across the floor, sever the spindles. Phase 2: shadow-clones of the Rudra of the Night of Ash wield your exact weapons.
She dies. She does not forgive.
SURPANAKHA-MAYA: I don't forgive you. Carry that. It is the only thing of mine you will ever keep.
Shard #4: the walls, as they truly were. A hill. A citadel. Small lights, hundreds, on the battlements, windows glowing with lamps. Washing lines. A child's toy by a gate.
Young Rudra, the sky bending above him. Kaala-Chakra beside him.
RUDRA (memory): Father. Who is inside?
KAALA-CHAKRA (memory): Killers.
RUDRA (memory): I see lights.
KAALA-CHAKRA (memory): Watchfires.
Two hundred paces down the hill, a child laughs. Rudra does not walk down. He raises his hands.
The memory ends.
RUDRA: I asked him.
SAVITRI: You asked the wrong person.
RUDRA: I could have gone and looked. It was two hundred paces.
Savitri says nothing.
Ferry Scene IV: Silence
Savitri rows. Rudra rows with her. Neither speaks for the length of the river.

CHAPTER FIVE: THE CELESTIAL COURT OF AMARAVATI (Debt of the Divine)
A shattered floating palace of marble and gold above storm-clouds. Permanent purple lightning. Apsara-assassins patrol. The gates are sealed.
SANJAYA: ~~Vishnu, who rules heaven.~~ Vishnu, who was locked out.
Mini-Boss: Uchchaihshravas, the Seven-Headed Nightmare Steed. Chain-lightning across a marble bridge.
Rudra enters the Grand Sabha. Rage rises in him. His brow flares, and the faces of the camp's dead flicker at the edges of the screen. Savitri's lantern dims.
Boss: Shumbha and Nishumbha, the Twin Regents.
Two throned figures, mirror-images. One in frost-armor, one in thunder.
SHUMBHA: Your father is up the hill. He sold you cheap.
NISHUMBHA: We bought him with a throne. He watched the camp burn with wine in his hand.
SHUMBHA: He did not even ask us to move the healers' tents.
RUDRA: Vishnu. Where is he?
NISHUMBHA: Your god? We locked him out ages ago. He is on the other side of the gate, knocking.
Fight. Phase 1: both brothers at once. Phase 2: when one falls, the other absorbs his twin's corpse and both shards, fusing into a four-armed Tempest Sovereign who splits the arena with lightning and ice.
The fusion is not grief. It is possession. He refuses to let his brother be gone.
Rudra defeats it. Shards #5 and #6.
Memory: the Council table. Wine, laughter, the twins pointing at the burning camp below. Kaala-Chakra stands very still, wine untouched. He is not looking at the fire. He is looking at one tent, the third from the east gate.
RUDRA: Why is he looking at that tent?
Savitri's lantern flickers.
SAVITRI: I do not know. I never knew.
Ferry Scene V
The only scene where Rudra speaks first.
RUDRA: What was she like?
Savitri does not answer for a long moment.
SAVITRI: She hummed while she worked. She stole sweets for her patients. She would have liked to be angry at you. She was not built for it.
She rows.
SAVITRI: She would have wanted you to stay.

CHAPTER SIX: THE SOUL FURNACE (Debt to His Family)
A cosmic lotus platform above a black-hole eclipse. The Furnace at its heart: a great machine of gears and burning souls, lighting heaven.
Penultimate Boss: The Hound of Doomsday.
A shadow with eyes. It has waited three thousand years. It cannot be killed. For three minutes the player survives. At the end it lies down.
HOUND: Not yet.
Rudra enters the Furnace chamber. His father stands at its centre, alive, ancient, third eye burning.
KAALA-CHAKRA: Rudra. My son.
RUDRA: You died. I saw you fall.
KAALA-CHAKRA: I let you think so. It was easier.
He gestures at the Furnace.
KAALA-CHAKRA: Look at what I built. Heaven lit by the dead. No more war, no more famine. Do you know what that took?
RUDRA: Forty thousand.
KAALA-CHAKRA: And you. I kept you.
Rudra says nothing. Then, the first question he has asked in three thousand years.
RUDRA: Where was Nachiketa?
A long pause. His father answers, cold, truthful.
KAALA-CHAKRA: In the third tent from the east gate. I chose it.
RUDRA: You left him there.
KAALA-CHAKRA: He found the ledgers. He would have taken the Furnace from me. He would have taken you.
RUDRA: You killed your own son.
KAALA-CHAKRA: I kept my other one.
RUDRA: You did not keep me.
KAALA-CHAKRA: You are here, aren't you?
He lifts his hand. The Furnace roars.
KAALA-CHAKRA: A weapon does not ask, Rudra. You asked me once, that night. And you took my answer. That was all I needed.
The Fight (three forms).
Phase 1, The Ascetic Guru: Telekinesis, eight golden chakrams orbit him, walls of spectral spears.
Phase 2, The Master of Astras: A celestial bow, meteor storms, clone archers, rotating laser mandalas.
Phase 3, Vishwarupa Kalantaka: He shatters the Amrita vat and mutates into a six-armed, ten-headed time-eater with a halo of golden skulls, bringing white fire to the outer ring, teleporting in four-hit chains, rewinding his own wounds unless you break his posture.
When the furnace cracks, the souls rise: pale, glowing, thousands of them. Among them, a young man's voice.
NACHIKETA (V.O.): Father.
One word. The old man freezes. His face, for one moment, is a father's. Then he reaches for the Wheel and rewinds, trying to put his son's voice back.
KAALA-CHAKRA: Not yet. Not yet. I have not finished.
He chooses power. Again.
Rudra breaks the final posture bar. He leaps onto his father's chest and tears the burning heart of the gem from his third eye.
KAALA-CHAKRA: (bleeding, reaching) If you kill me the sky falls.
RUDRA: Then let it.

THE CHOICE
The Mani is whole in Rudra's fist. It hums, warm, holding every soul he burned. The wound in his brow bleeds black. The game waits. No timer.
The gem cannot speak. It does not need to. Rudra knows what it is. It can heal him, but only by using them up.
Hold to crush. Release to keep.

ENDING ONE: THE VOW
Rudra closes his fist. The Mani shatters.
The souls rise, thousands of them, pale and bright, up through the broken ceiling into a sky that has not moved in three thousand years. Among them, two he knows: a girl of sixteen and a young healer, standing together, looking down.
The Furnace goes dark. Kaala-Chakra falls to his knees, dust spreading from his hands.
KAALA-CHAKRA: (quietly, to the sky) I was going to build a world with no endings.
He turns to dust, reaching.
Heaven's gates grind open.
Rudra walks down the mountain. The Hound follows. At the foot, the river. Savitri waits at her ferry, lantern low.
SAVITRI: The question.
RUDRA: Ask it.
SAVITRI: Will you still be here?
RUDRA: Yes.
She nods. The river takes its fare at last: the years she was owed. She ages them all at once, breathing out, light. She steps into the ferry, and the lantern goes out in her hands, finished.
SAVITRI: Ila.
Across the water, a girl's laugh. Savitri crosses.
The sun rises. Rudra kneels on the burned ground where the camp stood, the Gada across his knees, waiting for judgment. A conch sounds, far away. Footsteps on ash.
A figure in light stops beside him. Vishnu.
VISHNU: I told you to go and look.
RUDRA: I know.
Nothing else. The god does not leave. Neither does Rudra.
SANJAYA (V.O., uncorrected): Let it be written that he was not wronged. He was forgiven, and he was not let off. And he stayed.

ENDING TWO: THE SOVEREIGN
Rudra opens his hand. The Mani glows, golden. It heals him, spending the souls under it. The wound closes. The black stops. He feels, for the first time in three thousand years, whole.
The Furnace relights.
Rudra sits on his father's throne. The Wheel turns at his command. His voice, when he speaks, is his father's.
RUDRA: No more endings.
Far below, on a frozen river, a ferrywoman's lantern goes out.
The gates of heaven stay sealed. On the far side, a figure in light knocks once, twice, and falls silent.
SANJAYA (V.O.): There is a man on the mountain who cannot die...

