KALIYUGA: THE BLEEDING CROWN
The Full Story (v5)

Part One: The World Before
The war and the child
Long ago, heaven was at war with itself. The gods held it by right, and the asuras wanted it by force, and the asuras were winning. In the worst days, an old god who had almost nothing left gave all of it to a child not yet born. The child's mother, Suvarna, knew what the gift would cost and agreed. She died in the birth.
The boy was named Rudra. As soon as he drew breath, a gem opened in the middle of his forehead, the Mani, and with it came more power than a mortal body should hold. The gods called him their secret weapon. He did not know yet that he was one.
The father
Rudra was raised by his father, the sage Kaala-Chakra. In those years Kaala-Chakra was a good man: patient, wise, and gentle with children. He taught Rudra to fight, but he also taught him to be careful with the power he carried. Rudra loved him completely. He had no mother to love, so his father was everything.
Rudra also had a younger brother, Nachiketa, who did not take after either of them. Nachiketa was a gentle boy who grew up to be a healer. He bandaged birds as a child and treated wounded soldiers of both sides as a man. Rudra was the sword and Nachiketa was the one who repaired what the sword had done. The brothers adored each other.
The fall of Kaala-Chakra
The war dragged on. The asura twin kings, Shumbha and Nishumbha, saw that the gods' greatest weapon was guided by one man, and they went to him with an offer: wealth, a throne of his own, and a seat on the Council of Seven that ruled heaven. Kaala-Chakra said yes.
It did not happen all at once. A man who takes one compromise finds the next one easier. Kaala-Chakra stopped wanting the war to end, because the war had made him rich. He began telling Rudra that the gods were tyrants who had broken their promises. Rudra, who trusted him the way a son trusts his only parent, believed every word. The gods' secret weapon became the asuras' sword.
The lesson that mattered most came when Rudra was fifteen. A captured asura boy stood before them, and Kaala-Chakra held out a blade. Rudra asked: "He is a child, Father. Should I?" His father answered: "A weapon does not ask. It strikes." Rudra's hand shook, and he did it. That was the last time he ever questioned an order. Everything that followed grew from that moment.

Part Two: The Night of Ash
The lie
On the Eighteenth Night of the war, a sworn truce was signed. Kaala-Chakra came to Rudra with a shrouded body. He said it was Nachiketa and that the gods had murdered him under the truce. Rudra reached for the shroud, and his father put a hand on his wrist and said: "Please. Do not unwrap him. Remember him as he was." Rudra let go. He never lifted the cloth.
The body was not Nachiketa's. Nachiketa was alive, in the camp of healers, and had been there for days.
The rampage
Rudra's grief turned to rage. He stormed heaven's gate and killed hundreds, and it felt like pure power. Nothing could stand against him. Then his father came and told him where the killers were hiding: a citadel on a hill, within sight. Burn it, he said.
The question
Rudra looked at the hill and saw small lights on the walls. He asked his father: "Who is inside?"
"Killers," said Kaala-Chakra.
"I see lights."
"Watchfires."
Two hundred paces down the slope, a child laughed. Rudra did not walk down to look. He raised both hands and called down the Brahmashira, a weapon that burns a soul loose from rebirth. The citadel disappeared in one white breath.
It was not a citadel. It was the Shishira, a camp of healers, cooks, wounded soldiers and families, moved inside those walls that very week "for their protection" on Kaala-Chakra's orders. Forty thousand people died. Four Regents of the Council, who stood in Kaala-Chakra's way, died with them. Among the dead were Ila, a sixteen-year-old healer's apprentice and the daughter of the ferrywoman Savitri, and Nachiketa, who was in the third tent from the east gate. Kaala-Chakra had chosen that tent himself.
The god
Vishnu arrived too late to stop it. He found Rudra standing in the ash and fought him, but he did not fight to kill. He held back and spoke one thing, plainly: "Rudra. Go and look." Rudra, drunk on power and grief, did not hear it.
Vishnu broke the Mani into six shards and threw Rudra into the river of the dead. But Rudra could not die. The gem had caught the souls he burned, and as long as it held them, it held him. The god left him there, because he had no way to kill what the dead refused to let go.
What the father built
Kaala-Chakra took the seventh piece, the heart of the gem, from the wreck. He gave the six shards to people who had lost someone in the fire, telling each of them the same thing: your dead are inside this stone, and as long as you guard it, they are safe. It was a lie that kept six grieving people loyal to him for three thousand years. The twins took their shards by right.
At the top of the mountain he built the Soul Furnace. It burned the light of the trapped souls to power heaven: its lamps, its weapons, its gates, which he sealed so the gods could not return. He let it be believed that Vishnu ruled from the stolen throne. The truth was that Vishnu was locked out, and the war had never been about heaven. It was a harvest.
Kaala-Chakra also let Rudra believe he had died that night. It was easier.

Part Three: The Waking
Savitri
Three thousand years later, under an eclipse, a ferrywoman named Savitri hauls a body out of the river of the dead. It has a bleeding hole where a gem should be, and a broken mace in its fist.
Savitri is the only living mortal in the story. For three thousand years her river has been choked with ash from souls that cannot be reborn, and the river will not let her go while the dead have nowhere to cross to. She has aged only as far as the river allowed: worn, but not dead. Her lantern shows the true shape of illusions, mist and masks, but it burns her remaining years, so every use costs her.
She knows exactly who Rudra is. He is the man who killed her daughter. She pulls him out anyway, because he is the only one who can climb the mountain where her daughter's soul is held. Her words when he wakes: "Get up. You owe a lot of people."
Her bargain is simple: she will guide him to the top, and at the end he must answer one question. Will he still be here? He does not understand it. He agrees.
What Rudra believes
Rudra remembers the night as he edited it over three thousand years: a fortress of killers, no lights, no families, no question asked. He believes Vishnu broke him out of cruelty and that the god now rules heaven from a stolen throne. He believes his father died that night. His one purpose is to climb and make Vishnu pay.
A blind narrator, Sanjaya, tells the story, and Rudra's version is what Sanjaya was given: "Rudra, the betrayed, climbed from the river." As the truth comes out, Sanjaya's words are struck through and rewritten.

Part Four: The Climb
Each act is one thing Rudra owes. Each ends with a shard, a returned memory, and one more piece of the truth. The bosses come from Indian myth, but they are offices called Seats, filled by whoever fits the role. That is why figures from different epics can share one mountain.
Act One: The Charnel Plains (what he owes the dead)
A battlefield of rusted chariots, bone mountains and burning pyres, where the dead of both sides lie together. Savitri's rule is "I carry the dead. I do not fight for them." She guides and he fights.
He fights Dundubhi, a war-beast with a bronze drum for a chest, and then Vrikodara, the Iron Rakshasa, whom he once called brother. Vrikodara was away on the Night of Ash. His wife and son, healers in the camp, were not. His grief became a hunger that devoured his own clan. "Take the shard and look," he says.
As he dies, Vrikodara forgives him: "You did not know, brother. I could never hate you. That was what the hunger was for." It is cruelty disguised as mercy, because being forgiven is not being absolved.
The first shard returns a healer's tent, a Rakshasa child holding a lamp, and the lamp going out. Rudra says: "That could be any camp." Sanjaya's text corrects itself for the first time: the fortress of killers becomes a camp.
Act Two: The Drowned Lotus of Patala (what he owes the living world)
Caverns flooded with violet poison, the waste of the gods' war. The Nagas, once keepers of wisdom, have been milked for venom and mutated into blind monsters. Savitri's lantern becomes essential, because in the mist, only her light shows what is real.
Karkotaka, an invisible viper, attacks from behind four mirror illusions. Then comes Kadruja, queen of the Nagas, blinded after demanding justice for her son. Her youngest children were held as hostages under the truce, kept among the healers. She has not seen them in three thousand years.
The choice in her fight is the first real moral test: carry her eggs out of the arena one at a time under fire, or crush them for a faster win. If Rudra saves them, she gives up the shard willingly and lives, and a Naga flotilla joins him in the final act. If he crushes them, she dies screaming and the Nagas are lost. Neither is wrong, and both are remembered.
The shard returns Vishnu above him in the duel, and this time Rudra hears the words: "Rudra. Go and look." He says: "He said it." Savitri answers: "He did." Rudra asks why, then, the god broke him. She says: "Ask him when you find him." Sanjaya corrects himself again: cursed by Vishnu becomes stopped by Vishnu.
Act Three: The Forge of Vishwakarma (what he owes the workers)
A clockwork foundry inside a hollowed volcano, where enslaved smiths forge heaven's weapons. Rudra breaks their chains, and in thanks they reforge his broken mace. Their foreman says: "They told us the Undying One carries our tithe. We thought it was a story."
On the haft is a maker's mark: his father's. Rudra runs his thumb over it, grieving a man he believes is dead. Savitri does not look at him. She knows.
He fights a brass Garuda and then Shakti-Kavacha, a peerless spearman in golden armor grown into his flesh. Shakti-Kavacha's wounded soldiers were in the camp. He serves an oath he despises, and he wants Rudra to win. When he dies he says: "I swore an oath to a lie. You swore one to your grief. Look, Rudra. Look at what the furnace burns."
The shard returns the pyre: the shrouded body, and his father's hand on his wrist. Please. Do not unwrap him. Rudra realizes he never lifted the shroud. It is the second time he failed to look.
The war was never about justice. It was a harvest. Sanjaya corrects: the war for heaven becomes the harvest.
Act Four: The Weeping Banyan of Dandaka (what he owes the truth)
A continent-sized banyan grows upside-down from the clouds, its roots dripping golden sap and trapped memories. This is the emotional collapse of the game.
Among the faces in the roots, Rudra finds a girl of sixteen and recognizes Savitri's jaw. He turns on her. She does not deny it: she knew from the first hour, and she needed him to climb. He lowers his mace and falls to his knees. He offers to stop. She says no. "Finish it."
Sanjaya, too, breaks. He confesses that his words were given to him, that he never saw the camp or the lights, and that his version was commissioned. From here his narration is crossed out and rewritten line by line.
Maricha, a golden stag with antlers of mirrors, leads him off the path, and only the lantern shows which is real. Then comes Surpanakha-Maya, an ancient Rakshasi mutilated by the gods, who weaves the dream-banyan. Her daughter died in the camp. She is the only Warden who does not want pity, and she is right to hate him. "You keep asking what the god did to you," she says. "Ask what you did to me."
When she dies she refuses absolution: "I don't forgive you. Carry that. It is the only thing of mine you will ever keep."
The fourth shard returns the night as it really was. The hill, the citadel, the lamps in a hundred windows, washing lines, a child's toy by the gate. Young Rudra beside his father, asking: "Who is inside?" "Killers." "I see lights." "Watchfires." And the two hundred paces he never walked.
Rudra says: "I asked him." Savitri says: "You asked the wrong person." He says: "I could have gone and looked." She says nothing. The ferry crossing that follows is silent, the only one with no words.
Act Five: The Celestial Court of Amaravati (what he owes the gods)
A shattered floating palace above storm-clouds, with its gates sealed from inside. Rudra storms it for revenge, and his rage becomes a power: his brow ignites, and the faces of the camp's dead flicker at the edges of his vision. Each use dims Savitri's lantern.
A seven-headed steed guards the bridge. Then in the Grand Sabha waits Shumbha and Nishumbha, the twin kings who bought his father. They tell him what they did: "He sold you cheap. We watched the camp burn with wine in our hands. He did not even ask us to move the healers' tents." And when Rudra asks where Vishnu is, they laugh: "We locked him out ages ago. He is on the other side of the gate, knocking."
Rudra learns that the god he has hunted for three thousand years was never in heaven. Sanjaya corrects: Vishnu, who rules heaven becomes Vishnu, who was locked out.
When one twin falls, the other absorbs his corpse and both shards, fusing into one four-armed Tempest Sovereign. It is not grief. It is possession: he refuses to let his brother be gone. They loved only each other, and they paid for it with forty thousand lives. Rudra recognizes the shape of what they did.
The fifth and sixth shards return the Council table: wine, laughter, the twins pointing at the burning camp. Kaala-Chakra does not laugh. He stands very still, untouched wine in his hand, looking at one tent. Rudra does not yet understand why.
Fifth ferry crossing. This is the only one where Rudra speaks first, and he asks the question the whole game has circled: "What was she like?" Savitri tells him: Ila hummed while she worked, stole sweets for her patients, and would have liked to be angry at him but was not built for it. "She would have wanted you to stay."
Act Six: The Soul Furnace (what he owes his family)
A cosmic lotus platform above a black-hole eclipse, ringed by the grinding gears of the stopped world. The Hound of Doomsday guards the threshold. It is Rudra's own death, and it has waited for him three thousand years. It cannot be killed. For three minutes he survives its attack, and at the end it stops, lowers its head, and lies at his feet. It says one thing: "Not yet."
At the heart of the Furnace stands his father, alive.

Part Five: The Father
Kaala-Chakra greets him gently: "Rudra. My son." He admits he let Rudra believe he was dead, because it was easier. He shows him the Furnace, heaven lit by the dead, and says it is a world with no more war or famine. Rudra says the number: forty thousand. His father says: "And you. I kept you."
Then Rudra asks the first question he has asked in three thousand years: "Where was Nachiketa?"
His father answers, cold and truthful. "In the third tent from the east gate. I chose it." Nachiketa found the ledgers. He would have taken the Furnace, and he would have taken Rudra. "I kept my other one." Rudra says: "You did not keep me." His father says: "You are here, aren't you?"
He also says the line that explains everything: "A weapon does not ask, Rudra. You asked me once, that night. And you took my answer. That was all I needed."
The fight
Kaala-Chakra fights in three forms: the Ascetic Guru, who moves through telekinesis with golden chakrams orbiting him; the Master of Astras, who draws a celestial bow and fills the arena with meteors and rotating lasers; and finally a ten-headed, six-armed time-eater with a halo of golden skulls, fed by the Furnace.
When the Furnace cracks, the souls rise out of it, thousands of them, pale and glowing. Among them, a young man's voice says one word: "Father." For one moment the mask drops, and what is under it is a father. Then he reaches for the Furnace and tries to put his son's voice back. He chooses power over his son, again.
Rudra breaks the last posture bar, leaps onto his father's chest, and tears the heart of the gem from his third eye. Kaala-Chakra screams that if Rudra kills him the sky will fall. Rudra says: "Then let it."

Part Six: The Choice
The gem is whole in his fist. It holds every soul he burned. It can heal his wound, but only by using them up. The game waits with no timer, and the player decides: hold to crush it, or let go.
Ending One: The Vow
Rudra crushes the Mani. The souls rise out of it, thousands of them, into a sky that has not moved in three thousand years. Among them are a girl of sixteen and a young healer, standing together. The Furnace goes dark. Kaala-Chakra turns to dust, still reaching.
The gates of heaven grind open. Rudra walks down the mountain, the Hound at his heels. At the river, Savitri waits with her lantern.
"Will you still be here?"
"Yes."
The river takes its fare: the years she was owed. She ages them all in one breath, and the lantern goes out in her hands, finished. She steps into the ferry. Across the water, a girl laughs. Savitri crosses.
The sun rises for the first time in three thousand years. The curse that held Rudra is broken, so staying alive is now his choice. He kneels on the burned ground where the camp stood, his father's mace across his knees, waiting for judgment. A conch sounds, far away. A figure in light stops beside him.
"I told you to go and look," says Vishnu.
"I know," says Rudra.
Neither leaves. Sanjaya narrates the last lines, uncorrected: Let it be written that he was not wronged. He was forgiven, and he was not let off. And he stayed.
Ending Two: The Sovereign
Rudra keeps the gem. It heals him, spending the souls under it. The wound closes and the black stops. He feels whole for the first time in three thousand years.
The Furnace relights. Rudra sits on his father's throne, and when he speaks it is in his father's voice. On a frozen river far below, a ferrywoman's lantern goes out. At the sealed gate of heaven, a figure in light knocks once, twice, and falls silent. Sanjaya begins again: There is a man on the mountain who cannot die...

What the Story Is About
Rudra spends three thousand years blaming others for his ruin, and discovers he committed an unforgivable crime. His father lied to him, and he chose not to look. Revenge cannot undo the past, power cannot bring the dead back, and forgiveness does not erase responsibility.
The thing Rudra fights all the way up the mountain is himself, the part that refuses to check what it believes. He was given a hundred chances to look. A god told him to. A child laughed two hundred paces away. A shroud lay in front of him. Each time, he trusted the one voice that told him what he wanted to hear.
The ending asks the same question of the player. When the gem is in your hand and the choice is yours, do you free the people you hurt and stay to answer for it, or do you take the power and become the man who taught you not to ask?
